3. Dependencies: license elections, license texts and source code

Some dependencies are offered under more than one license. This project elects
the following. The Java versions are the ones resolved by the Gradle build
(runtimeClasspath) of this release and the npm version is the one locked in
webui/package-lock.json; the license choices are those declared in each project's
POM, source files or package.json.

  - logback-classic and logback-core 1.5.38 are offered under EPL-2.0 or
    LGPL-2.1-only. Elected: LGPL-2.1-only. The text of that license is the file
    licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt and logback's copyright notice is the file
    licenses/logback-NOTICE.txt; both are also in META-INF/licenses of the
    executable jar.
  - jakarta.annotation-api 3.0.0 is offered under EPL-2.0 or GPL-2.0 with the
    Classpath Exception. Elected: GPL-2.0 with the Classpath Exception.
  - jakarta.mail-api 2.1.5 and angus-mail 2.0.5 are offered under EPL-2.0 or
    GPL-2.0 with the Classpath Exception: their source files and their
    META-INF/NOTICE.md say so, and their jars carry both texts. (Their POMs also
    list EDL-1.0, which those files do not grant, so it is not elected.) Elected:
    GPL-2.0 with the Classpath Exception.
  - DOMPurify 3.4.16 (the web console) is offered under MPL-2.0 or Apache-2.0.
    Elected: Apache-2.0.

The Java libraries are shipped unmodified, each as its own jar under
BOOT-INF/lib of the executable jar, so a user can replace one with a modified
version. DOMPurify is bundled, unmodified, into the console's JavaScript.

Source code for the five Java libraries above (the two logback jars,
jakarta.annotation-api, jakarta.mail-api and angus-mail) travels with every
binary release: the build task :service:copyleftSources collects their -sources
jars, and scripts/publish-artifact.sh puts them into the directory
third-party-sources/ of the deployment bundle and of the artifact directory next
to starry-api.jar, so they are offered from the same place as the binaries. They
are also available from the projects (https://logback.qos.ch/,
https://github.com/jakartaee/common-annotations-api,
https://github.com/jakartaee/mail-api, https://github.com/eclipse-ee4j/angus-mail)
and from Maven Central under the same coordinates. The source code of DOMPurify is
at https://github.com/cure53/DOMPurify (its Apache-2.0 election has no source
obligation).

All other Java and npm dependencies are offered under permissive licenses
(Apache-2.0, MIT, MIT-0, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, ISC, CC0-1.0 and the Eclipse
Distribution License 1.0, which has the terms of BSD-3-Clause), and any choice
they offer is between permissive licenses. The Java build checks this: it stops
when a library declares only licenses outside that list (or none) and is not one
of the elections above, and when a jar carries no license text and declares a
license other than Apache-2.0, MIT, MIT-0 or CC0-1.0. Many of the Java jars carry
no license file of their own. The build therefore generates
JAVA-THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt from the Maven POMs of the runtime classpath: it
lists every library with its version and the license it declares, and prints the
Apache-2.0 text and, for the jars that declare MIT and carry none, the MIT text
(it is the file META-INF/JAVA-THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt inside the executable jar,
at the root of the deployment bundle, and
service/build/generated/java-notices/JAVA-THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt after
"./gradlew :service:generateJavaNotices"). Jars that do carry license and notice
files keep them under BOOT-INF/lib. The web console build writes the license
text and notice of every npm package that ends up in the console to
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt, and stops when a bundled package has none. That file is
served next to the console (it is the file
BOOT-INF/classes/static/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt inside the executable jar and
webui/dist/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt after "npm run build"). Because the console
displays a copyright line while it runs, that file also ends with the notice for
logback: its copyright line, the LGPL-2.1-only election and where the license text
is (section 6 of the LGPL-2.1 asks for a copyright notice and a reference to the
license among what a running work displays).

